r F7 extends it.  \n- Black has G4–F5 diagonal (slope -1). E6 is White’s stone, so Black can’t extend that diagonal past E6.  \n\nActually, looking carefully: Black’s diagonal G4(7,4)–F5(6,5) could continue to E6(5,6), but E6 is White, so Black’s diagonal is blocked.  \n\nTherefore, White’s priority is to build its own shape. The strongest shape is to place a stone that makes two open ends for future 3-in-a-row.  \n\nLet’s choose **D6** (adjacent to E6 horizontally and diagonally to G5? No, not aligned).  \n\nBetter: **F4** blocks Black’s vertical pair F5–F6 from becoming three, and also is diagonally (slope 1) aligned with G5–F4–E3? Not helpful yet.  \n\nActually, the most common good opening move here is to play near the existing stones to create a “two” that can become a three. Let’s check **D7**: diagonally with G5? No.  \n\nLet’s instead consider **H6**: makes a horizontal pair with G5 (G5–H6), and also is vertical with nothing yet.  \n\nBut **D5** makes a diagonal two with E6 (E6–D5–C4) and also is horizontal with E6? No, E6 is row 6, D5 row 5.  \n\nGiven the small number of stones, the most solid move is to **block Black’s potential vertical line** while starting a new line for White.  \nBlack’s most dangerous: vertical at F5–F6, so F4 or F7.  \nBetween them, **F4** is better because it’s closer to other White stones? Actually G5 is at (7,5), so F4(6,4) is diagonally adjacent to G5 (slope -1: G5 to F4 is (7,5)→(6,4)). Yes! That’s a diagonal pair for White: F4–G5. Also F4 blocks Black’s vertical.  \n\nSo F4 creates a White diagonal two and blocks Black’s vertical two.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nF4 serves both defensive (stopping Black’s vertical three) and offensive (making a diagonal two with G5) purposes. It’s in an open area, so it has potential to extend in multiple directions later.  \n\n**Answer:**  \n{"col": "F", "row": 4}'}
