nes: G5 (O), G6 (O) with G4 (X), G7 (X) blocks vertical.  \n\nBetter idea: look at diagonal / from E6 (O): D5 empty, C4 empty, etc. But not enough stones.  \n\nCheck diagonal \\ from F4 (O): G5 (O), H6 (O), I7 (X) — blocked.  \n\nSo no immediate 5-in-a-row.  \n\n**Creating an open 3 or 4 threat:**  \nConsider move at **D6**:  \n- Horizontal: D6, E6 (O), F6 (X) blocks — not good.  \n- Diagonal /: D6, E5 empty, F4 (O) — not strong.  \n\nConsider move at **H5**:  \n- Diagonal /: H5, G6 (O), F5 (X) — blocked.  \n- Diagonal \\: H5, G4 (X) — blocked.  \n\nConsider move at **I6**:  \n- Horizontal: I6, H6 (O), G6 (O), F6 (X) — blocked.  \n\nActually, let’s check **F7**:  \n- Vertical: F7, F6 (X) — no.  \n\nBest positional move seems to be **D7** or **D5** to build from E6 (O) and G5 (O).  \n\nBut looking carefully:  \nBlack’s I7 (X) and G7 (X) — they might be building something.  \nWhite should block or create own threat.  \n\nPotential strong move: **H5**  \nWhy? It connects to G5 (O) and H6 (O) diagonally \\? Wait, H5 and G5 are vertical neighbors? No, H5 is right of G5? Let’s check coordinates: G5 (col G, row 5), H5 (col H, row 5) — horizontal neighbors.  \nSo H5, G5, F5 (X) — blocked.  \n\nBetter: **D5**  \nD5 connects diagonally / to E6 (O) and F4 (O)? Wait: D5 (row5), E6 (row6), F4 (row4) — not same diagonal.  \n\nActually: D5, E6, F7 empty — that’s diagonal \\. Could be a 3-in-a-row diagonal if we play D5. Then next move C4 or F7 for 4.  \n\nBut immediate win? No.  \n\nGiven the dispersed stones, no immediate 5-in-a-row exists.  \nSo I must choose a move that strengthens my position while blocking Black.  \n\nBlack’s possible threat: I7 (X) and G7 (X) with H7 empty — if Black plays H7, that’s 3-in-a-row horizontally with I7, H7, G7, but F7 empty — not yet 4.  \n\nWhite’s best: Play **H7** to block that Black horizontal and also connect to H6 (O) vertically.  \n\nCheck: H7 (O), H6 (O), H5 empty — potential vertical threat later. Also H7 connects diagonally / to G6 (O)